            <h3 class="heading-md" style="color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 0.75rem; font-size: 1.25rem;">
              Weighted Grade Calculator vs. Average Grade Calculator
            </h3>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
              Many students mistakenly calculate their marks using a simple <strong>average grade calculator</strong>, which treats every single test, homework assignment, and quiz as having equal importance. However, the majority of modern syllabi rely on a weighted category system.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
              When using our <strong>weighted grade calculator</strong>, you can use a <strong>grade calculator with weights</strong> to assign proportional importance to different categories:
            </p>
            <ul style="padding-left: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem;">
              <li><strong>Percentage Weighting:</strong> Standard university format where homework might be worth 20%, midterms 30%, and the final exam 50%. Each score is multiplied by its category percentage weight.</li>
              <li><strong>Total Points System:</strong> If your instructor uses a points-based curriculum, switch seamlessly to <strong>grade calculator points</strong> mode to calculate marks based on points earned divided by total possible points.</li>
              <li><strong>Drop Lowest Policies:</strong> Many professors allow students to drop their lowest quiz or homework score. Our <strong>assignment grade calculator</strong> handles this automatically so your calculated mark reflects true course policies.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              By relying on a dedicated <strong>class grade calculator</strong> rather than a basic mean score calculator, you ensure that high-value midterms and laboratory reports receive their proper numerical influence.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Section 3: Final Exam Target -->
          <div>
            <h3 class="heading-md" style="color: var(--color-ink); margin-bottom: 0.75rem; font-size: 1.25rem;">
              Using the Final Exam Grade Calculator to Target Your Desired Grade
            </h3>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
              As the end of the term approaches, the most common question students ask is: <em>"What score do I need on my final exam to get an A?"</em>
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
              Our built-in <strong>final exam grade calculator</strong> uses the proven algebraic formula:
            </p>
            <div style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.875rem; background: var(--color-canvas-subtle); border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline); padding: 0.75rem 1rem; border-radius: var(--radius-sm); margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--color-ink);">
              Required Final Score = (Desired Overall Grade &minus; Current Class Grade &times; (1 &minus; Final Weight)) / Final Weight
            </div>
            <p>
              This <strong>finals grade calculator</strong> compares your current grade against the entire grading scale in an instant Scenario Comparison Matrix. Whether you are using this as an <strong>exam grade calculator</strong> for a single midterm or an <strong>overall grade calculator</strong> for a critical capstone course, the live feasibility badges tell you whether your target is comfortable, challenging, or requires extra credit.
            </p>
          </div>

